WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.125 --> 00:00:04.922
- [Michael] This facility was an old mine.

00:00:04.922 --> 00:00:07.839
It was operated for about 125 years,

00:00:09.465 --> 00:00:13.632
and these elevators are
basically a mile in depth.

00:00:16.051 --> 00:00:18.288
And now we're in the process
of converting and operating

00:00:18.288 --> 00:00:20.560
this as a deep underground
science facility,

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:24.727
really trying to unlock the
secrets of the universe.

00:00:27.672 --> 00:00:28.505
- [Female Narrator] In the middle of

00:00:28.505 --> 00:00:29.722
South Dakota's Black Hills,

00:00:29.722 --> 00:00:33.101
lies the Sanford Underground
Research Facility.

00:00:33.101 --> 00:00:35.215
Here, physicists are
conducting experiments

00:00:35.215 --> 00:00:37.752
that have the potential
to alter our understanding

00:00:37.752 --> 00:00:40.717
of how the universe was created.

00:00:40.717 --> 00:00:42.138
- Our lab is unique in that

00:00:42.138 --> 00:00:43.819
we are the deepest underground facility

00:00:43.819 --> 00:00:46.982
for doing this type of
work in the United States.

00:00:46.982 --> 00:00:48.973
Really, the goal is to try and limit

00:00:48.973 --> 00:00:50.961
any sort of impurities that
can get into the experiment

00:00:50.961 --> 00:00:55.079
that would cause problems
with our data collection.

00:00:55.079 --> 00:00:56.338
- We're a mile underground

00:00:56.338 --> 00:00:59.078
because that layer of
rock and dirt protects us

00:00:59.078 --> 00:01:00.908
from cosmic rays that are impinging

00:01:00.908 --> 00:01:02.761
on the surface of the earth.

00:01:02.761 --> 00:01:06.182
A number of cosmic rays down
here is effectively zero,

00:01:06.182 --> 00:01:09.099
especially compared to the surface.

00:01:10.615 --> 00:01:12.645
- One of our experiments
that we have in the lab

00:01:12.645 --> 00:01:14.677
is trying to unlock
questions about particles

00:01:14.677 --> 00:01:17.361
that are fairly new in our
understanding of the universe,

00:01:17.361 --> 00:01:18.611
like neutrinos.

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:22.528
- In any given second,
about 60 billion of them

00:01:22.528 --> 00:01:25.129
pass through your thumbnail.

00:01:25.129 --> 00:01:27.651
The irony is that this
experiment studies neutrinos

00:01:27.651 --> 00:01:30.415
without ever detecting one.

00:01:30.415 --> 00:01:32.494
We are looking for a radioactive decay

00:01:32.494 --> 00:01:34.096
that can only take place

00:01:34.096 --> 00:01:37.929
if the neutrinos play a
certain role in there.

00:01:39.862 --> 00:01:43.435
We collect about a
terabyte of data a week.

00:01:43.435 --> 00:01:45.714
You couldn't have taken this much data,

00:01:45.714 --> 00:01:48.759
let alone analyzed it 20 years ago

00:01:48.759 --> 00:01:52.592
so it's very exciting
from that point of view.

00:01:54.324 --> 00:01:55.526
- [Female Narrator] It is the team's hope

00:01:55.526 --> 00:01:58.446
that this abandoned mine
which once took from earth

00:01:58.446 --> 00:02:01.764
will now help us understand
our place in the universe.

00:02:01.764 --> 00:02:03.550
- If we understand the neutrino,

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:05.296
we can potentially understand

00:02:05.296 --> 00:02:08.221
why the galaxies and the
stars and the planets

00:02:08.221 --> 00:02:11.598
evolved the way they did
and why they're here.

00:02:11.598 --> 00:02:14.531
- We have one Nobel Prize
under our belt already

00:02:14.531 --> 00:02:16.280
and we're hoping that the experiments

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:17.230
that we have here now

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:21.397
will lead to additional
Nobel Prizes in the future.

